nformation of complex texts on both concrete and abstract topics in written texts in social and work contexts.", "type": "secondary_5"}, {"content": "Understands the main ideas and specific information of complex texts on both concrete and abstract topics in written texts in social and work contexts.", "type": "secondary_6"}, {"content": "Understands the main ideas and specific information of complex texts on both concrete and abstract topics in written texts in social and work contexts.", "type": "secondary_7"}, {"content": "Understands the main ideas and specific information of complex texts on both concrete and abstract topics in written texts in social and work contexts.", "type": "secondary_8"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "brightness", "type": "name"}, {"content": "the quality or state of giving out or reflecting light", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(noun)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: Judging by their brightness, they could be millions of light years away. ", "type": "example"}, {"content": "engenn10le01ob07aud01", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word_describer", "contents": [{"content": " These are words you use to talk about astronomy!", "type": "text"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "adventurous", "type": "name"}, {"content": "willing to try new or difficult things", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(adjective)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: I have children and they  aren't the adventurous type.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "engenn10le01ob07aud02", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "to reconsider ", "type": "name"}, {"content": "to \u200bthink again about a \u200bdecision or \u200bopinion and \u200bdecide if you \u200bwant to \u200bchange it", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(verb)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: There could be a chance for them to reconsider.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "engenn10le01ob07aud03", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "distant", "type": "name"}, {"content": "far away", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(adjective)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: I wonder what it's like to live in a distant galaxy.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "engenn10le01ob07aud04", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "to speculate", "type": "name"}, {"content": "to \u200bguess \u200bpossible \u200banswers to a \u200bquestion when you do not have enough \u200binformation to be \u200bcertain ", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(verb)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: There are theories out there that speculate that life could exist in some planets like Mars.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "engenn10le01ob07aud05", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "learning_points", "contents": [{"content": "<span class=\"italic\">Must</span>, <span class=\"italic\">Might</span>, <span class=\"italic\">Could</span>, <span class=\"italic\">May</span> and <span class=\"italic\">Can't</span> to Speculate about the Present", "type": "heading"}, {"content": "You can use special words called <span class=\"italic\">modal verbs</span> when you want to make speculations about events or situations in the present. <br>Speculating means that you think about something and make guesses about it.", "type": "describer"}, {"content": " There are different degrees of probability for each modal verb. Depending on how sure you are of what you want to say, you will need to choose a different modal verb.", "type": "text"}, {"content": " When you are almost certain, you use <span class=\"italic\"><span class=\"strong\">must</span></span>. This is used for the most logical interpretation.<br> When you are less certain, you use <span class=\"strong\"><span class=\"italic\">may</span></span> or <span class=\"italic\"><span class=\"strong\">might</span></span> or <span class=\"italic\"><span class=\"strong\">could</span></span>. These modal verbs all have the same meaning.<br> When you are making a negative speculation, you use <span class=\"strong\"><span class=\"italic\">can't</span></span>. This means you are almost certain that something doesn't happen.<br>", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Judging by their brightness, they <span class=\"strong\">could</span> be millions of light years away. ", "type": "example"}, {"content": "There <span class=\"strong\">must</span> be tons of research on how to do that in the library.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "This <span class=\"strong\">could</span> be the start of something really special.", "type": "example"}]}, {"type": "learning_points", "contents": [{"content": "<span class=\"italic\">Will</span>/<span class=\"italic\">Won't</span> for Speculation vs. <span class=\"italic\">Will</span>/<span class=\"italic\">Won't</span> for Strong Disapproval or Refusal", "type": "heading"}, {"content": "You can use <span class=\"italic\">will</span> or <span class=\"italic\">won't</span> to express different things. One meaning is for making guesses about something in the present and another is to show refusal or disapproval.", "type": "describer"}, {"content": "Compare the sentences below.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "<span class=\"italic\">My cellphone is ringing late at night. That <span class=\"strong\">will</span> be my friend calling me back. It <span class=\"strong\">won't</span> be my mother calling me at this time.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "<span class=\"italic\"><span class=\"strong\">If</span> you<span class=\"strong\">'ll</span> eat sugary snacks every day, it's no surprise that you've gained weight. <br> <span class=\"strong\">If</span> you <span class=\"strong\">won't</span> do the dishes, then go to your room as punishment.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "How do you think the meaning of each sentence is different?", "type": "text"}, {"content": "In the first sentence, you use <span class=\"italic\"><span class=\"strong\">will</span></span> to show that you are sure that it is your friend returning your phone call. You are also sure that you mother is not the person on the phone. <br><br> In the second sentence, you use <span class=\"italic\"><span class=\"strong\">if + will</span></span> to show insistence. This sentence means \u201cIf you insist on eating sugary snacks every day, it's no surprise that you've gained weight\u201d. You are showing that someone is firm about intending to do something, like \u201ceating sugary snacks\u201d in this example. <br><br> In the third sentence, you use <span class=\"strong\"><span class=\"italic\">if + won't</span></span> to show refusal. Here, the person doesn't want to do the dishes.  ", "type": "text"}, {"content": "<span class=\"strong\">If</span> I<span class=\"strong\">'ll</span> go to Mars, you'll come too, right? ", "type": "example"}, {"content": "<span class=\"strong\">If</span> they <span class=\"strong\">won't</span> go, it's hard for me to leave them.", "type": "example"}]}, {"content": "engenn10le01ob07re1img01.jpg", "type": "background_image"}, {"type": "takeaway_extension", "contents": [{"content": "With a partner list all the necessary things we need to sustain human life on earth.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Imagine that you and your partner have a spacecraft and are taking a trip to the moon.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Speculate about which items are the most important and explain why.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Write a list of  no more than 8 items to bring.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Share your list with another group.", "type": "text"}]}, {"type": "takeaway_assessment", "contents": [{"content": "I talk about science and leisure.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "I speculate about different events.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "I write a list of objects.", "type": "text"}]}]}